<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gordon Guggisberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Frederick Gordon Guggisberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Gordon Guggisberg was a Canadian-born British army engineer who was governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> from 1919 to 1927. His ten-year development plan built Takoradi harbour, railways, roads and the Korle Bu Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>, and he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achimota-school">Achimota School</a>. He also opened senior posts in the colonial civil service to Africans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg.jpg" alt="Gordon Guggisberg" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Guggisberg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gordon_Guggisberg_full_length_1922.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick Gordon Guggisberg was born in Galt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, on 20 July 1869, the eldest son of the merchant Frederick Guggisberg and Dora Louisa Willson; his grandfather Samuel had emigrated from Uetendorf in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Bern in 1832. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1879, and he was educated at Burney's School in Portsmouth and the Royal Military Academy, Woolwich. Commissioned into the Royal Engineers in 1889, he served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> from 1893 to 1896 and from 1897 taught fortification at Woolwich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1902 Guggisberg surveyed the Gold Coast Colony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>, becoming director of surveys there in 1905. In 1910 he became director of survey in Southern Nigeria, where his handbook for his assistants forbade unpaid labour and required that goods be paid for, and in 1913 surveyor-general of Nigeria. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded divisional engineers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-somme">Battle of the Somme</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, and received the Distinguished Service Order in 1918.</p><p>As governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> from 1919 he extended the railways and built the deep-water harbour of Takoradi, which replaced surf-boats for handling traffic, and in 1923 he commissioned Korle-Bu Teaching Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. Convinced by his survey work that Africans could reach the development of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> given better education, he founded Achimota College to train African teachers. He aimed to develop the country by and for its people rather than for European capitalists.</p><p>In 1928 he became governor of British Guiana, where he introduced drastic administrative reforms, worked on the drainage and irrigation on which its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> depended, and promoted immigration and peasant settlement, but failing health forced him to leave in 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Guggisberg died at Bexhill-on-Sea, Sussex, in 1930, at the age of 60. His second wife, the actress Decima Moore, had accompanied him on his survey journeys, and their joint book We Two in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> (1909) describes that life. In 1973, on the 50th anniversary of Korle-Bu, the Ghanaian government raised a statue of him, a rare tribute from a post-colonial government to a colonial governor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gordon Guggisberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cambridge, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in Bexhill-on-Sea, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-80.31&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Cambridge on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2616 626 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gordon Guggisberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3066 896h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3066 896h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-80.31&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
